title:
Do Cuttlefish Dream of Electric Shrimp? A Natural History Note on Behavioral Quiescence Strategies in Wild European Cuttlefish (
 Sepia officinalis
 )

abstract:

The red fox (
Vulpes vulpes
) is a major invasive predator in Australia, and camera traps are increasingly used to monitor it and evaluate control programs. These devices generate large datasets, but turning them into abundance estimates requires statistical and programming skills that many land managers lack. Detection data are therefore often reduced to naïve counts that ignore detection probability. We developed FoxWatch, a Shiny R application that estimates fox abundance with Simultaneous‐Count Models through a guided point‐and‐click interface. It sorts detection records into 10‐day blocks and fits the model by Markov Chain Monte Carlo sampling, returning abundance estimates with quantified uncertainty. We tested FoxWatch on 5 years of data from 35 camera traps at Oolambeyan National Park, Australia. It processed the dataset in under 5 minutes, versus 2–3 hours by hand. FoxWatch lowers the analytical barrier so practitioners without programming skills can assess fox abundance and support predator management.

We study non-interacting Poissonian run-and-tumble particles (RTPs) in two dimensions whose velocity orientations are controlled by an arbitrary circular distribution $Q(\phi)$. RTP-type active transport has been reported to undergo localization inside crowded and disordered environments, yet its non-equilibrium dynamics, especially at intermediate times, has not been elucidated analytically. Here, starting from the standard (one-state) RTPs, we formulate the localized (two-state) RTPs by concatenating an overdamped Brownian motion in a Markovian manner. Using the space-time coupling technique in continuous-time random walk theory, we generalize the Montroll-Weiss formula in a decomposable form over the Fourier coefficient $Q_{
u}$ and reveal that the displacement moment $\left \langle \mathbf{r}^{2\mu}(t) ight angle$ depends on finite angular moments $Q_{
u}$ for $|
u|\leq \mu$. Based on this finding, we provide (i) the angular distribution of velocity reorientation for one-state RTPs and (ii) $\left \langle \mathbf{r}^{2}(t) ight angle$ over all timescales for two-state RTPs. In particular, we find the intricate time evolution of $\left \langle \mathbf{r}^{2}(t) ight angle$ that depends on initial dynamic states and, remarkably, detect hyperdiffusive scaling $\left \langle \mathbf{r}^{2}(t) ight angle \propto t^{ eta(t)}$ with an anomalous exponent $2< eta(t)\leq 3$ in the short- and intermediate-time regimes. Our work suggests that the localization emerging within complex systems can increase the dispersion rate of active transport even beyond the ballistic limit.

title:
Modelling and performance analysis of a continuous (
 R,s,S
 ) inventory model

abstract:
This work aims to analyze an inventory system controlled by an (
R,s,S
) policy, whose stored product is described by a random continuous variable. We first propose to model the state of the considered system using a non-homogeneous discrete Markov chain (DMC) defined at inspection dates, without any restriction on the nature (Markovian or non-Markovian) of the processes governing the system. In addition, the special case where the processes governing the system are Markovian is examined. The resulting model simplifies to a homogeneous DMC, allowing for a more tractable numerical analysis. Then, an economic model associated with such systems was derived. Finally, our results were illustrated with numerical examples.

